          <SocialProof users="24.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#before" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Before You Start</a>
          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Preparing the Surface</a>
          <a href="#product" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Choosing the Product</a>
          <a href="#first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The First Coat</a>
          <a href="#second" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Second Coat</a>
          <a href="#buff" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Buffing and Curing</a>
          <a href="#fix" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Fixing a Hazy Finish</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealing granite is not painting it. The product has to sit on the stone long enough to travel into the pores, and every trace that has not gone in by the end has to come off — that is the entire skill, and almost nobody does the second half.
        </p>

        <div id="before" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Start, Confirm It Needs It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do the water test first. A quarter cup of water left on a clean, dry area for fifteen minutes will tell you whether the stone absorbs at all, and a meaningful share of granite countertops do not. Sealing a non-porous slab leaves product drying on the surface as a film, which is more work to remove than sealing was to apply. The full diagnostic is in our guide to <a href='/how-to-tell-if-granite-needs-sealing/'>telling whether granite needs sealing</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check also that the countertop is granite rather than engineered quartz. Quartz is bound in resin, is non-porous by construction, and must never be sealed. The tell is pattern uniformity: real granite has visible mineral crystals, fissures and variation across the slab, while quartz repeats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deal with any existing stains before sealing, not after. An impregnating sealer over an absorbed oil mark makes that mark permanent, because the sealer's job is to stop things entering and leaving the pore structure. Draw stains out with a poultice, let the area dry completely, and only then proceed — the method is in our guide to <a href='/how-to-remove-stains-from-granite/'>removing stains from granite</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, pick your moment. The countertop needs to be out of use for the dwell, the second coat and the cure — realistically the whole afternoon and evening, with no water on the surface for at least 24 hours afterwards. Sealing on a Saturday morning before a dinner party is how corners get cut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Preparing the Surface Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear everything off the countertop, including the things that live there permanently. The knife block, the kettle and the fruit bowl all have a footprint of unsealed stone underneath them, and they are also the first things to get put back onto a surface that has not finished curing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean with a stone-safe cleaner or a dilute solution of washing-up liquid in warm water, then rinse thoroughly with clean water and wipe down. What you are removing is not visible dirt so much as the film of cooking oil, polish and cleaner residue that accumulates on any kitchen surface. That film blocks the sealer from reaching the stone, and it is the single most common reason a sealing job does not last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid anything acidic, anything containing ammonia, and any general-purpose spray cleaner with additives. Vinegar and lemon-based cleaners are the ones to keep well away — they are marginal for granite at the best of times and they leave the surface chemistry in no state to accept a sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then let it dry completely. Completely means several hours, not a wipe with a tea towel: a solvent-carried impregnator cannot displace water in the pores, so any residual moisture blocks penetration exactly where you want it most. Overnight after cleaning is the safe interval, and a fan in the room shortens it considerably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Choosing the Right Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            You want an impregnating sealer, sometimes sold as a penetrating sealer, rated for food-contact surfaces. It works by depositing a water and oil repellent inside the pore structure while leaving the stone's appearance and breathability unchanged. What you do not want on a kitchen countertop is a topical sealer that forms a film, which will wear unevenly, scratch and eventually need stripping.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Carrier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Life on Granite</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fluoropolymer impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best oil repellency, strong fumes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fluoropolymer impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low odour, slower penetration</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane / siloxane impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent or water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good on water, weaker on oil</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour-enhancing impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Darkens stone — permanent decision</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Topical acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not appropriate for countertops</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Solvent-carried products penetrate denser stone better and generally last longer, at the cost of real fumes — open windows, turn off any pilot light, and use the extractor. Water-based products are easier to live with and a reasonable choice on absorbent light granites where penetration is not the limiting factor. Our tested picks across both are in the <a href='/best-granite-sealer/'>best granite sealer guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colour-enhancing sealers deserve a warning. They deepen the stone the way water does when it is wet, and on some granites the effect is beautiful. It is also effectively permanent, uneven if applied carelessly, and impossible to reverse without professional resurfacing. Test on an offcut or inside the sink cutout before committing the whole kitchen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The First Coat and the Dwell
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work in sections of roughly two to three square feet. Apply the sealer generously with a clean lint-free cloth, a foam applicator or the product's own spray, keeping the surface visibly wet across the whole section. This is not a thin even coat like paint — the stone needs a reservoir of product sitting on it to draw from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then leave it. The dwell time is where the sealing actually happens, and most labels specify somewhere between five and fifteen minutes. During that window the carrier is transporting the active repellent down into the pore structure, and cutting it short leaves the sealer in the top fraction of a millimetre where ordinary cleaning removes it within months.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PART EVERYONE RUSHES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep the surface wet for the full dwell time, then remove every trace that has not soaked in.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Top up any area that dries early rather than letting it flash off. When the dwell ends, buff the surface completely dry with a clean cloth. Sealer left to dry on top of the stone is the hazy film people blame on the product.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Watch for sections drying early. Absorbent stone will drink the first application in two or three minutes, and dry patches are stone that has stopped receiving product. Top them up with more sealer so the whole section stays wet for the full dwell. On a thirsty granite the first coat can take two or three top-ups, which is normal and is why the first coat uses far more product than the second.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the dwell, buff the section completely dry with a clean lint-free cloth before moving on. Do not let one section sit while you apply the next — sealer that dries on the surface is far harder to remove than sealer wiped off wet, and on a large island that mistake is easy to make twice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="second" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Second Coat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most impregnating sealers specify a second coat, and on absorbent granite it makes a measurable difference to how long the protection lasts. The interval is usually 30 minutes to two hours after the first — long enough for the carrier to flash off, short enough that the surface is still receptive. The label's number wins over any general rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second coat goes on exactly like the first, but it will use considerably less product, because the pores are now partly occupied. Expect to use around half to two thirds of what the first coat took. If the second coat goes on and the stone drinks it as fast as the first did, the slab is unusually absorbent and a third coat is justified — this is one of the few sealing jobs where three is sometimes correct.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the dwell honest on the second coat too. It is tempting to treat it as a token pass and wipe it off after two minutes, but the second coat is doing the work of filling what the first could not reach. Same wetting, same timer, same thorough buff at the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay particular attention to the edges, the bullnose and the sink cutout on the second coat. Those faces were cut and polished separately, they are frequently more absorbent than the top surface, and they are also where water lands most. A first coat applied by wiping across the top rarely wets them properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Buffing, Curing and Getting the Kitchen Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buff the entire countertop with a clean, dry, lint-free cloth once the final coat's dwell has ended, and then go over it again ten minutes later with a fresh cloth. The second pass catches product that has wicked back out of the pores onto the surface, which is common on dense stone and is exactly what produces a streaky finish if left.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cure time is not the same as dry time. The surface will feel dry within an hour, but the sealer continues reacting for considerably longer — typically 24 hours before water exposure and up to 72 hours for full chemical cure. Keep the countertop dry for at least a day, and put nothing heavy or absorbent on it in the meantime.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Timeline</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dwell per coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30-120 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Between coats</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before water contact</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">48-72 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Full cure</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Verify the result once it has cured. Run the same water test you started with: a quarter cup on a flat area for fifteen minutes. It should leave no mark at all. If it still darkens, the stone either needed a third coat or the dwell was too short, and either way another coat now is trivial compared with discovering the problem through a stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then write the date on the bottle and put it somewhere you will find it. Knowing what went on and when turns the next maintenance coat into a straightforward job — our guide to <a href='/how-often-to-seal-granite-countertops/'>how often to seal granite</a> covers the intervals worth planning around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. If You End Up With a Hazy Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cloudy, streaky or slightly tacky surface after sealing means product dried on top of the stone instead of being buffed off. It is the most common sealing complaint and it is an application error rather than a product fault. It is also entirely fixable, provided you catch it within the first day or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to re-dissolve the residue with the same carrier the sealer used. For a solvent-based product, apply a little more of the sealer itself to the hazy area, let it sit for a minute to soften the dried film, then buff hard with a clean dry cloth. It sounds counterintuitive to add more product, but the fresh solvent is what lifts the dried residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a water-based sealer, warm water and a non-abrasive stone cleaner with firm buffing will usually clear it. Do not reach for a scouring pad or a household abrasive cream — granite is hard but the polish on it is not indestructible, and a dulled patch is considerably worse than a hazy one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the residue has cured for weeks, it may need a dedicated stone sealer remover. At that point it is worth a call to your fabricator rather than experimenting on a visible surface — and it is worth remembering that the whole problem is avoided by buffing thoroughly at the end of each dwell rather than at the end of the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating It Like Painting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin even coat wiped on and left is the wrong method entirely. The stone needs a reservoir of product sitting on it for the full dwell time so the carrier can transport the repellent into the pores. Keep the section visibly wet and top up any area that dries early.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting the Dwell Short</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wiping the sealer off after two minutes leaves it in the top fraction of a millimetre, where ordinary cleaning removes it within months. Set a timer for the label's dwell and honour it on both coats, including the one that feels like a formality.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Buffing Off the Excess</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every trace that has not soaked in by the end of the dwell has to come off, or it dries as a hazy, slightly tacky film. Buff each section dry before moving on, then go over the whole surface again ten minutes later with a fresh cloth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Damp Countertop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A solvent-carried impregnator cannot displace water in the pores, so residual moisture blocks penetration precisely where it matters. Clean, then leave the surface to dry for several hours — overnight is the safe interval — before opening the sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Edges and Sink Cutout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cut and polished separately, those faces are often more absorbent than the top and they take the most water. Wiping across the top surface never wets them properly. Do them deliberately on both coats.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you seal granite countertops step by step?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean with a stone-safe cleaner and let the surface dry for several hours. Apply impregnating sealer generously to a two to three square foot section, keep it visibly wet for the label's dwell time of five to fifteen minutes, then buff the section completely dry. Repeat across the countertop, apply a second coat after 30 to 120 minutes, and keep the surface dry for 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does granite sealer need to sit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Five to fifteen minutes per coat, depending on the product. That dwell is when the carrier transports the repellent into the pore structure, so it is the part of the job that matters most. Top up any section that dries early rather than letting it flash off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you wipe off granite sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and thoroughly. Anything that has not soaked in by the end of the dwell must be buffed off with a clean dry cloth, then checked again ten minutes later for product that has wicked back to the surface. Sealer left to dry on top is the hazy film people blame on the product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of granite sealer should I apply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two is standard. The first coat takes most of the product because it is going into open pores; the second uses roughly half to two thirds as much. On unusually absorbent stone that drinks the second coat as fast as the first, a third is justified.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can use the countertop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The surface is dry to the touch within an hour, but keep it free of water for 24 hours and avoid heavy or absorbent items for the same period. Full chemical cure takes 48 to 72 hours with most impregnators, and the sealer is not at full strength until then.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my granite look cloudy after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealer dried on the surface instead of being buffed off. With a solvent-based product, apply a little more sealer to the hazy area, let it soften the residue for a minute, and buff hard with a dry cloth. Water-based residue usually clears with warm water, a stone cleaner and firm buffing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal granite myself or should I hire someone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a straightforward DIY job on a normal kitchen — the skill is in the dwell and the buff, not in anything requiring tools. Professional sealing makes sense for very large installations, for colour-enhancing products where a mistake is permanent, or where stains need drawing out first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

